<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/path-data-polyfill@1.0.10/path-data-polyfill.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;div class="grd"&gt;
  &lt;div class="col"&gt;
    &lt;h3&gt;SVG transform functions&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;svg id="svg0" viewBox="0 0 100 100"&gt;
      &lt;!-- inherited transforms --&gt;
      &lt;g style="transform:scale(1.75) translate(-20px, -30px) rotate(15deg)"&gt;
        &lt;g transform="rotate(45 10 10) scale(0.5 1) skewX(10)" transform-origin="center"&gt;
          &lt;path id="path0" d="M 50 25 a 1 1 0 010 50 1 1 0 010 -50" /&gt;
        &lt;/g&gt;
      &lt;/g&gt;
    &lt;/svg&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class="col"&gt;
    &lt;h3&gt;Hard coded coordinates&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;svg id="svg2" viewBox="0 0 100 100"&gt;
      &lt;path id="path2" d="" /&gt;
    &lt;/svg&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class="col"&gt;
    &lt;h3&gt;SVG output&lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;textarea id="svgOut"&gt;&lt;/textarea&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>The above example illustrates:</p>
<ul>
<li>once we have normalized path data solely relying on x/y coordinate pairs we can easily calculate the transformed positions by looping through all x/y value pairs</li>
<li>existing browser APIs significantly facilitate the matrix retrieval – also respecting inherited transforms from ancestor groups as well as normalizing a mix of SVG attribute based transforms and CSS properties</li>
<li>converting transforms to hard coded coordinates increases the markup size – unless we apply additional optimizations such as rounding, converting to relative commands and apply shorthands when possible</li>
</ul>
<p>All in all: the required processing steps sum up – it's up to you whether you prefer to write your own helper library or rely on existing ones.</p>
<p>I highly recommend to take inspiration from existing libraries:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://github.com/fontello/svgpath" rel="nofollow noreferrer">svgpath</a>: one of the most complete and performant SVG path data parsers – also providing transformations for tedious arc commands</li>
<li>Timo Kähkönen's <a href="https://gist.github.com/timo22345/9413158" rel="nofollow noreferrer">github gist</a></li>
</ul>

Q: 2 blocks are still reachable in loss record cs50 dictionary.c A: <p>In analyzing and testing your code, I actually think the issue raised is referencing the call to the unload function.  In testing out your dictionary program code, the output looked nominal.  However, when I added in a node counter and a print statement to output how many nodes were freed, it showed no memory being freed up before the program exited.</p>
<pre><code>bool unload(void)
{
    int counter = 0;
    node *head = NULL;
    node *cursor = head;        /* cursor has been also set to NULL                         */
    // freeing linked lists
    while (cursor != NULL)      /* cursor is NULL - code inside the loop will not execute   */
    {
        node *temp = cursor;
        cursor = cursor-&gt;next;
        free(temp);
        counter++;
    }
    printf(&quot;Number of nodes freed: %d\n&quot;, counter);
    return true;
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Spell_5/bin/Release$ ./Spell_5 Stuff.txt Tests.txt

MISSPELLED WORDS

aaaaa
ideal
lllll
nope
state
Number of nodes freed: 0    &lt;----

WORDS MISSPELLED:     5
WORDS IN DICTIONARY:  20
WORDS IN TEXT:        10
TIME IN load:         0.00
TIME IN check:        0.00
TIME IN size:         0.00
TIME IN unload:       0.00
TIME IN TOTAL:        0.00
</code></pre>
<p>What is happening is the node pointer variable &quot;cursor&quot; is being set to the same value as the &quot;head&quot; pointer variable, which was initialized to &quot;NULL&quot;.  Thus the while loop is never entered.</p>
<p>With that in mind, following is a minor refactoring of the dictionary load function, and a refactoring of the unload function with code derived from analysis of this same issue from other entries, keeping the spirit of your original code intact.</p>
<p>In the dictionary load function, an additional statement is added when a new node allocation occurs and the dictionary word is stored.  Right after that storage, the &quot;next&quot; node pointer is initialized to a value of &quot;NULL&quot; to ensure proper functionality later on in the unload function and also to impress on the fact that there are no subsequent node references at this node level.</p>
<pre><code>    // Copies word into node if malloc succeeds
    strcpy(new_node-&gt;word, word);
    new_node-&gt;next = NULL;          /* Ensure tidy linked list pointers for the unload function     */
</code></pre>
<p>Then, as noted from analysis of other similar dictionary issues, following is a refactored version of the unload function.</p>
<pre><code>bool unload(void)
{
    int counter = 0;
    // TODO
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; HASHTABLE_SIZE; i++)
    {
        node *cursor = hashtable[i];
        while (cursor)                  /* Only process hash table entries that were populated  */
        {
            node *tmp = cursor;
            cursor = cursor-&gt;next;
            free(tmp);
            counter++;
        }
    }

    printf(&quot;Nodes structures freed: %d\n&quot;, counter);

    return true;
}
</code></pre>
<p>With those two bits of refactoring, following is the terminal output from a retest using the refactored code.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/Spell_5/bin/Release$ ./Spell_5 Stuff.txt Tests.txt

MISSPELLED WORDS

aaaaa
ideal
lllll
nope
state
Nodes structures freed: 20      &lt;---- matches number of words in the dictionary

WORDS MISSPELLED:     5
WORDS IN DICTIONARY:  20        &lt;---- number of words in the dictionary
WORDS IN TEXT:        10
TIME IN load:         0.00
TIME IN check:        0.00
TIME IN size:         0.00
TIME IN unload:       0.00
TIME IN TOTAL:        0.00
</code></pre>
<p>The bit to point out is that in this example, twenty words were loaded in from a simple dictionary, and once the other functionality was executed, the memory for those twenty words was unloaded.</p>
<p>The takeaway from this is if unsure about the functionality of ones code, either one can use a debugger to step through code where there might be questions, or in a simpler way, some temporary print statements can be added at suspected points in the code to highlight what might be amiss.</p>

Q: Making an array that accepts only a string and instances of a class? A: <p>You can use the base class <code>DialogueCommand</code> as a container for a string and use implicit conversion operator:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>class DialogueCommand
{
    public readonly string str;
    protected DialogueCommand() { str = string.Empty; }
    protected DialogueCommand(string str) { this.str = str; }
    public static implicit operator DialogueCommand(string str)
        =&gt; new DialogueCommand(str);
}

class SetImage  : DialogueCommand { public SetImage (string str) : base(str) { } }
class PlayMusic : DialogueCommand { public PlayMusic(string str) : base(str) { } }
class StopMusic : DialogueCommand { }
class SetSmooth : DialogueCommand
{
    protected readonly bool val;
    public SetSmooth(bool val) { this.val = val; }
}

// ...

return new DialogueCommand[] 
{
    &quot;this is an example line with two commands!&quot;,
        new SetImage(&quot;image3&quot;),
        new PlayMusic(&quot;example&quot;),
    &quot;line without commands&quot;,
    &quot;&quot;, new SetSmooth(false),
    &quot;and that's how the lines are made!&quot;,
        new StopMusic()
};
</code></pre>
